# The ledger of `html`: what Python's `html`, `html.entities` and
# `html.parser` (3.15.2), Go's `html` (1.27.3) and `golang.org/x/net/html`
# (v0.49.0, read from this machine's module cache with `go doc -all`, no
# network) let a program do, and BeautifulSoup 4 and html5lib, the yardstick
# the author named on 2026-11-16, one block per capability, and what Rebel
# answers. Rust's standard library has no HTML, so its line is `-` in every
# block. The Python and Go lists are this machine's, 2026-11-16: `dir` of the
# three modules and of `HTMLParser`, and `go doc -all` of the two packages.
# BeautifulSoup and html5lib are not installed on this machine and nothing was
# installed: their entries are their documented API, named as their
# documentation names them, and none of their answers was observed.
#
# The parser is written from the WHATWG HTML Living Standard's text, fetched
# from html.spec.whatwg.org on 2026-11-16 (parsing.html, scripting.html,
# semantics-other.html). That text has moved past what Go's x/net/html and
# html5lib implement: processing instructions are read as nodes, `select` holds
# whatever it is given (the "in select" modes are gone), `search` is special.
# The tests were written by hand from the standard, its own examples of
# 13.2.10 among them, in html5lib-tests' tree format, since html5lib is not
# importable here. Beside them, 179 inputs (misnested formatting, tables and
# foster parenting, templates, frameset, foreign content and its breakouts,
# doctypes, references) were read by this package and by Go's x/net/html
# v0.49.0, run offline from the module cache, and the two trees compared in
# that format on 2026-11-16: 177 agree, and the 2 that differ are where Go
# departs from the standard's text (it keeps a repeated attribute, 13.2.5.33
# drops it; it reads `</>` as a comment, 13.2.5.7 reads it as nothing). Inputs
# where the 2026 standard moved past Go (`select`, processing instructions)
# were left out of that comparison and are tested by hand.

capability html/tree  a document held as a tree of nodes a program can match on and walk
  heroes  html.Document, html.Node, html.Space, html.AttributeSpace, html.Attribute, html.node_of, html.is_element, html.ROOT
  go      x/net/html: Node, Node.Type, Node.Data, Node.DataAtom, Node.Namespace, Node.Attr, NodeType, ErrorNode, TextNode, DocumentNode, ElementNode, CommentNode, DoctypeNode, RawNode, Attribute, Attribute.Namespace, Attribute.Key, Attribute.Val
  rust    -
  python  BeautifulSoup, Tag, Tag.name, Tag.attrs, NavigableString, Comment, Doctype, ProcessingInstruction, CData, Declaration; html5lib's etree and dom trees
  note    the tree is held flat and its nodes numbered, as Go links its nodes; a `match` on a `Node` answers its seven cases. A template's contents are a `fragment` node, its one child, as html5lib-tests print them. `==` compares two documents with no recursion. Decided 2026-11-16

capability html/parse  a whole page read as a browser reads it, never failing
  heroes  html.parse, html.default_options, html.parse_with, html.ParseOptions, html.Parsed
  go      x/net/html: Parse, ParseWithOptions, ParseOption
  rust    -
  python  BeautifulSoup(markup, "html5lib"), html5lib.parse, html5lib.HTMLParser, HTMLParser.parse
  note    the standard's tokenizer and tree construction, every insertion mode, the adoption agency and foster parenting, as the 2026 standard writes them. Go's Parse refuses a page nested past 512; here nothing fails on what a text holds. Python's `html.parser.HTMLParser` builds no tree and recovers by its own rules, not the standard's. Reading takes time linear in the text since 2026-11-18, repaired after `parse_fragment` read 1 MB in 0.78 s and 4 MB in 7.24 s: 45 places in the reader and one in the selectors' where a list grew through a record's field, copied whole at every piece (a text's pieces, a comment's, an attribute's name and value, a table's pending text, the parse errors, a selector's groups), now grown in place through an `@` parameter; each error's column counted from its line's start, now in one sorted pass; each attribute looked for in a walk of its tag's, now in a map of names. Measured on this Mac in user seconds, one program before and after: a quarter of a megabyte of character references 25.60 s before and 0.08 s after (1 MB past 120 s, then 0.33 s; 4 MB 1.36 s), of stray end tags on one line past 60 s and 0.23 s (4 MB 3.84 s), of one tag's attributes 14.77 s and 0.08 s. Found then and repaired the same day, after: thousands of formatting elements alike but for their attributes (`<b id=1>x<b id=2>x`, 8,000 of them 4.56 s), where the Noah's Ark clause (13.2.4.3) walked the whole list at every push, and three alike far back (`<b class=k>` three times for each of 2,000 kinds, 2,000 others, then each kind once more), where the earliest was found by a walk and taken out by moving every entry after it. Now each entry carries a number and its kind (name, namespace, attributes in any order, and the markers before it), the numbers of each kind after the last marker are kept, at most three, the earliest is left `GONE` where it stands, and the list is compacted once those are half of it. Measured on this Mac in user seconds, best of three: 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000 alike but for their attributes 0.32, 1.25 and 5.03 s before (the same files read 0.30, 1.17 and 4.56 then) and 0.02, 0.04 and 0.08 s after; the far case for 2,000 and 4,000 kinds 7.13 and 27.97 s before, and for 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000 kinds 0.10, 0.21 and 0.43 s after. Every tree the reader built over 4,600 generated pages heavy with formatting elements, markers and misnested end tags is the same bytes before and after

capability html/parse-fragment  markup read inside an element, as `innerHTML` reads it
  heroes  html.parse_fragment, html.ParseOptions
  go      x/net/html: ParseFragment, ParseFragmentWithOptions
  rust    -
  python  html5lib.parseFragment, HTMLParser.parseFragment
  note    the context element by name and namespace; the tokenizer starts in the state the standard gives that element, so inside `title` or `textarea` all is text

capability html/parse-errors  every parse error of a page, with where it stands
  heroes  html.parse_with, html.ParseError
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  html5lib HTMLParser.errors, HTMLParser(strict=True)
  note    the tokenizer's errors by the standard's own names; tree construction's, which the standard does not name, by eight names of the package's own; each with its line and column, the column in characters. A strict reading that fails at the first error is not offered: a program reads `errors.len() == 0`

capability html/parse-limits  the length and depth a reading allows, set by the caller
  heroes  html.ParseOptions, html.LIMIT, html.DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH
  go      x/net/html: Tokenizer.SetMaxBuf, ErrBufferExceeded
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    past `max_bytes` the reading fails `limit`, the standard saying nothing of length; past `max_depth` an element is put beside its parent, as Chromium does past 512, since the standard leaves the depth to the browser. Go fails a page past 512 deep

capability html/scripting  a page read as with scripting enabled or disabled
