capability html/tokenize  the tokens of a page read one by one, with no tree
  heroes  refused: tokens without tree construction are not what a browser reads (`</br>`, a `p` inside a `p` and a table's stray text mean other things once built), and the tree is read in one pass of the text's length; a token stream would be a second way to read with none of the standard's recovery
  go      x/net/html: Tokenizer, NewTokenizer, NewTokenizerFragment, Tokenizer.Next, Tokenizer.Token, Tokenizer.TagName, Tokenizer.TagAttr, Tokenizer.Text, Tokenizer.Raw, Tokenizer.Err, Tokenizer.AllowCDATA, Tokenizer.Buffered, Tokenizer.NextIsNotRawText, Token, Token.Type, Token.Data, Token.DataAtom, Token.Attr, Token.String, TokenType, ErrorToken, TextToken, StartTagToken, EndTagToken, SelfClosingTagToken, CommentToken, DoctypeToken, TokenType.String
  rust    -
  python  html.parser.HTMLParser, HTMLParser.feed, HTMLParser.close, HTMLParser.reset, HTMLParser.getpos, HTMLParser.get_starttag_text, HTMLParser.handle_starttag, HTMLParser.handle_endtag, HTMLParser.handle_startendtag, HTMLParser.handle_data, HTMLParser.handle_entityref, HTMLParser.handle_charref, HTMLParser.handle_comment, HTMLParser.handle_decl, HTMLParser.handle_pi, HTMLParser.unknown_decl, HTMLParser.convert_charrefs

